Parquet: a row group is decoded one block at a time, so queries that need only a few row groups leave threads idle; decoding its blocks in parallel lanes makes ClickBench Q38 (0-based) 21% faster

### Describe the situation
The Parquet reader decodes a row group one block at a time, because each column chunk has a single page cursor. Queries whose rows sit in a few row groups therefore leave most decoding threads idle. The patch lets the reader's unused threads decode later blocks of the same row group in parallel.

One-shot `clickhouse local`, 100 ClickBench Parquet files in page cache, 16 Graviton3 cores, 10 paired runs; medians, 95% CI.

| Query (0-based, as on the ClickBench site), 16 threads | master, ms | patched, ms | Change (95% CI) | CPU time |
|---|---:|---:|---|---:|
| Q38 `URL` of `CounterID = 62`, `IsLink <> 0` | 55 | 43 | −21.0% (−26.8%, −14.8%) | +5% |
| Q41 `WindowClientWidth/Height` of `CounterID = 62` | 42 | 36 | −13.1% (−20.0%, −5.8%) | +7% |
| Q37 `Title` of `CounterID = 62` (peak RSS 315 → 353 MB) | 65 | 58 | −9.2% (−16.3%, −1.4%) | +11% |
| Q42 `DATE_TRUNC('minute', EventTime)` of `CounterID = 62` | 36 | 33 | −8.2% (−14.3%, −1.7%) | +3% |
| Q27 `AVG(length(URL))` by `CounterID` | 600 | 574 | −4.6% (−6.9%, −2.3%) | +1% |
| Q20 `count() WHERE URL LIKE '%google%'` | 484 | 460 | −4.4% (−7.0%, −1.6%) | +1% |
| Q36 `URL` of `CounterID = 62` (peak RSS 466 → 528 MB) | 85 | 85 | +1.5% (−4.8%, +8.2%) | +14% |
| Q1, Q12, Q39, Q40, W2, W3 | | | no significant change (−7.4% to +0.9%) | −6% to +6% |
| W1 `sum(cityHash64(*)) WHERE CounterID = 62` (wide read, no lanes) | 182 | 184 | −0.5% (−2.7%, +1.7%) | 0% |
| 4 concurrent clients, each Q36–Q42 and Q20 (batch wall) | 3450 | 3540 | +1.3% (−2.3%, +5.0%) | +3% |
| Cold page cache: Q37, Q38, Q40, Q41, W1 (3 runs) | | | −2.4% to 0%; Q41 −7.6% from one slow master run | |

The same 16 cores with 32 and 64 threads (oversubscribed):

| Query, threads | master, ms | patched, ms | Change (95% CI) | CPU time | Peak RSS, MB |
|---|---:|---:|---|---:|---:|
| W1, 32 threads | 193 | 196 | +0.8% (−5.1%, +7.1%) | 0% | 1017 → 1025 |
| W1, 64 threads | 202 | 202 | −0.2% (−3.4%, +3.2%) | +1% | 1032 → 1038 |
| Q38, 32 threads | 63 | 52 | −15.4% (−21.8%, −8.4%) | +5% | 329 → 337 |
| Q38, 64 threads | 68 | 58 | −13.3% (−15.8%, −10.7%) | +7% | 337 → 350 |
| Q37, 32 threads | 69 | 65 | −3.2% (−7.6%, +1.5%) | +14% | 324 → 370 |
| Q37, 64 threads | 78 | 70 | −11.1% (−15.3%, −6.7%) | +13% | 335 → 389 |
| Q36, 32 threads | 95 | 90 | −6.5% (−10.2%, −2.7%) | +14% | 478 → 559 |
| Q36, 64 threads | 94 | 87 | −9.0% (−11.5%, −6.4%) | +16% | 492 → 577 |
| Q20, 32 threads | 492 | 462 | −6.8% (−8.9%, −4.7%) | +1% | 3333 → 3377 |
| Q20, 64 threads | 518 | 491 | −5.1% (−7.0%, −3.1%) | +2% | 4185 → 4355 |

Row groups read in lanes (`ParquetRowGroupsReadInLanes`) at 16, 32 and 64 threads: W1 none of 4; Q36–Q42 3 of 4; Q20 5, 7 and 14 of 325.

Q38 is line 39 of ClickBench's `queries.sql`. The Q39 discussed in #125738 is Q39 here, without significant change.

<details><summary>Why it happens and what the patch changes</summary>

`ReadManager` reads the row subgroups (blocks) of a row group strictly one after another: every column chunk has one cursor, so block n+1 starts only after the last step of block n. In ClickBench's 100-file layout the `CounterID = 62` rows of the dashboard queries Q36–Q42 are in 4 row groups of 3 files; after the other 97 files are pruned, those readers keep 1–2 threads busy on a 16-core machine.

The patch splits such a row group into lanes, at most 4, and no more than the reader's column data thread share as if it were the only reader (4 at 16 threads, 1 below 5 threads). Each lane has its own column chunk cursors and dictionaries and takes the next block that no lane has taken, so cursors only move forward. Lanes share the prefetched bytes and decompress each dictionary page once; a data page that several lanes read at the same time is also decompressed once. The blocks of a row group can therefore be delivered out of order. That is allowed without `input_format_parquet_preserve_order`; with that setting on, lanes are off.

A lane is added only when the reader seems to have a spare thread. This is a best-effort check without a lock, against the reader's share of the query's parsing threads, not against idle CPUs of the machine:

- the reader's current column data thread limit is not taken by tasks in progress and groups of tasks waiting to be scheduled, and
- one block cannot already use those threads: in the block's most expensive step, the columns' total uncompressed size divided by the largest column's must be below 2, and below the reader's current thread limit.

The fixed bound of 2 keeps wide reads out of lanes at any thread count: W1's four row groups have 2.5–4.3. With only the thread-limit check, which grows with `max_parsing_threads`, W1 got lanes in 3 of 4 row groups at 64 threads and took 12.7% longer, with 25% more CPU and 27% more peak RSS. The cap of 4 lanes keeps the extra memory per row group at its 16-thread size.

Who gets lanes at default settings: local reads of files without a page index, with at least 5 parsing threads, few row groups left after pruning, and blocks whose decoding is dominated by one column. With the default `input_format_parquet_use_offset_index = 1`, a column chunk that has an offset index is read page by page and keeps one lane. ClickHouse writes that index by default (`output_format_parquet_write_page_index = 1`), so files written by ClickHouse don't use lanes; ClickBench's files have no page index. Object-storage reads with column data read-ahead keep one lane, and so do row groups that the columns cache fills or serves.

Lanes are also off where order or per-row-group state matters: top-K row group bookkeeping, lazy materialization row sets and repeated (array) columns. A new ProfileEvent `ParquetRowGroupsReadInLanes` counts row groups read in lanes, and test `05491_parquet_row_group_lanes` compares a lane read with a single-threaded one.

Page CRCs are now verified only for pages whose values are read, in every read, with or without lanes: a data page skipped by the row check is no longer verified. The dictionary page CRC is verified once per chunk.

Correctness: the full pre-`LIMIT` results (`count()`, `sum(cityHash64(*))` of each query without `ORDER BY`/`LIMIT`) of all 43 ClickBench queries are identical on master and patched. Two 2M-row, single-row-group files with 8 KiB pages (`UInt64`, nullable, dictionary and plain strings) give identical results for 10 filters × 2 block sizes at 16 threads (twice) and 1 thread, with lanes active in all 40 cases. Timed outputs differ only where the query ends with `LIMIT` over tied counts (Q38–Q41), and they vary within each binary as well.

Cost: more blocks are decoded at once, so a fresh process touches more new memory. At 16 threads, one-shot CPU time grows 3–14% on the dashboard queries and peak RSS by up to 13%; at 32 and 64 threads, CPU time by up to 16% and peak RSS by up to 17%. Q36 pays +14% CPU and +13% peak RSS at 16 threads without a significant latency gain: its `GROUP BY URL` on one stream, not decoding, limits it, and the gate cannot detect that. Wide reads, cold reads and 4 concurrent clients are unchanged; scans are unchanged or slightly faster.

Memory accounting: each lane decodes its own dictionaries, which, like master's single dictionary, are outside the reader's per-stage memory accounting. While the next block to deliver is unread, the scheduler runs the queued tasks of all lanes of that row group past the memory watermark, so the overshoot can be up to 4 blocks instead of one.

</details>

### Which ClickHouse versions are affected?
<details><summary>Tested versions and source commits</summary>

Master 607c2a3dca0028f8b753b776eb00352445c957be (2026-11-02), Release build, clang-21, no LTO, both binaries from the same build tree. The patch is the diff below on that commit. It applies unchanged to master 7f11488e0a55c537637ba65dab8852c757374718 (2026-11-04). The Parquet reader files it edits are identical there; the reader changes merged since the base (#128607, footer prefetch for object storage sources; #125134, `DELTA_BINARY_PACKED` header checks) are not on the local-file path measured here.

</details>

### How to reproduce
<details><summary>Data, queries and measurement procedure</summary>

Data: ClickBench `hits_0.parquet` … `hits_99.parquet` (athena_partitioned, 14,737,666,736 bytes), read once beforehand so they are in the page cache. Host: r7g.4xlarge (16 Graviton3 cores, 123 GiB).

Each run is a new process with the official ClickBench view, one query and 16 threads:

```sh
taskset -c 0-15 clickhouse local --tmp --time --format=Null \
  --max_threads=16 --max_parsing_threads=16 \
  --query="$(cat create.sql); $QUERY"
```

`create.sql` is ClickBench's `clickhouse-parquet-partitioned/create.sql`; queries are ClickBench's `queries.sql`, numbered from Q0 (Qn is line n+1; Q36–Q42 are the `CounterID = 62` dashboard queries). The extra controls:

```sql
-- W1, a wide read:
SELECT count(), sum(cityHash64(*)) FROM hits WHERE CounterID = 62;
-- W2:
SELECT count(), sum(cityHash64(*)) FROM hits WHERE UserID = 435090932899640449;
-- W3:
SELECT count(), sum(cityHash64(WatchID, JavaEnable, GoodEvent, EventTime, EventDate, CounterID, ClientIP, RegionID, UserID, CounterClass, OS, UserAgent, IsRefresh, ResolutionWidth, ResolutionHeight, ResolutionDepth, FlashMajor, NetMajor, UserAgentMajor, CookieEnable)) FROM hits WHERE URL <> '';
```

Procedure: 10 rounds; in each round every query runs once per binary, rotating the binary order between rounds and queries. The binaries are fresh `dd` copies made right before the session. The statistic is the `--time` query time of each run; ratios are paired by round, with a Student-t interval on the log ratios. CPU time comes from `perf stat` task-clock, peak RSS from `/usr/bin/time`.

The 32- and 64-thread runs use the same command with `--max_threads` and `--max_parsing_threads` set to 32 or 64, still under `taskset -c 0-15`, so they oversubscribe the 16 cores; they are not a run on a 32- or 64-core machine.

Concurrency: 4 processes start at once, each runs Q36–Q42 and Q20 in sequence; 8 rounds with alternating binaries. Cold: `sync; echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches` before each run (binary re-cached), 3 rounds.

To see whether lanes were used: `ParquetRowGroupsReadInLanes` in `--print-profile-events`.

</details>

### Expected performance
<details><summary>Expected behavior</summary>

When a query needs only a few row groups and the reader has unused decoding threads, those threads should decode further blocks of the same row group instead of waiting. Queries that already keep the threads busy (scans of many row groups, wide reads, concurrent load) should be unaffected.

</details>

### Related issues and pull requests
<details><summary>Related work</summary>

- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/issues/124139: each read stage gets 1/5 of the parsing threads. Lanes work within the reader's share and would get more threads with that change.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/125738: reads one file by several sources, one per group of row groups, but cannot split a single row group. When its sources get at most one parsing thread each (buckets ≥ `max_parsing_threads`), they decode in the pipeline threads; the lane cap is then 1, so lanes stay off for those reads.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/123890: would enable the columns cache by default. Lanes stay off for row groups that the cache fills or serves, so with that change they would apply only to row groups the cache doesn't admit.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/125275: rebalances after multi-file reads; Q36 and Q39 are limited by aggregating on one stream rather than by decoding.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/issues/129108: filtering PREWHERE columns in place should lower the extra memory of lanes (not measured separately).

</details>

