OPTIMIZE FINAL writes the merged part with stale metadata after waiting for running merges

`OPTIMIZE FINAL` on `MergeTree` reads its metadata snapshot once, before waiting for running merges. A `RENAME COLUMN` that commits while it waits publishes new metadata and registers its mutation. The retried selection sees the mutation and records its version in the merged part, but the part is written with the old metadata: it claims the rename while storing the old column. The renamed column then reads back as defaults.

`StorageMergeTree::merge` takes `metadata_snapshot` outside the retry loop of `selectPartsToMerge` (the `on_wait_for_running_merges` path). On the retry the selection has to use the metadata that matches the mutations it records.

**Repro** (stateless test with fail points; fails on master, `sum(b)` reads 0)

```bash
# two parts; max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool = 1 keeps background merges away
CREATE TABLE t (id UInt64, a UInt64, PROJECTION cnt (SELECT count())) ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY id
    SETTINGS max_bytes_to_merge_at_max_space_in_pool = 1, min_bytes_for_wide_part = 0;
INSERT INTO t SELECT number, 1 FROM numbers(100);
INSERT INTO t SELECT number + 100, 1 FROM numbers(100);

SYSTEM ENABLE FAILPOINT merge_task_projection_stage_pause;
OPTIMIZE TABLE t PARTITION tuple() FINAL;              # (1) holds both parts, parked in the projection stage
SYSTEM ENABLE FAILPOINT mt_select_parts_to_mutate_max_part_size;   # the rename must not run as a mutation
OPTIMIZE TABLE t PARTITION tuple() FINAL;              # (2) waits for (1): "Waiting for currently running merges"
INSERT INTO t SELECT number + 200, 1 FROM numbers(100);
ALTER TABLE t RENAME COLUMN a TO b SETTINGS alter_sync = 0;
SYSTEM DISABLE FAILPOINT merge_task_projection_stage_pause;        # (1) finishes, (2) retries and merges
SYSTEM DISABLE FAILPOINT mt_select_parts_to_mutate_max_part_size;

SELECT count(), sum(b), countIf(b = 1) FROM t;
```

Expected: `300 300 300`. Actual: `b` is 0 for the rows of the part merged by (2).

Related: https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/122934

Join reordering puts 14-20M rows into hash tables in TPC-DS q4, q11 and q74: the UNION ALL filter materialize('s') = 's' is estimated as 1% selective

### Describe the situation
TPC-DS q4, q11 and q74 filter a UNION ALL constant column. The selected branch gets `materialize('s') = 's'` next to `d_year = 2001`; the estimator counts it as 1% selective, so join reordering builds the sales sides (14-20M rows in total), not `customer`. The patch evaluates such comparisons.

TPC-DS SF10 MergeTree, default settings (automatic statistics), 4-vCPU arm64, ThinLTO off; median of 10 paired runs, 95% CI of the ratio.

| Query | Base | Patched | Change (95% CI) | Hash table rows | Peak memory |
|---|---:|---:|---|---|---|
| q74 | 1393 ms | 1022 ms | -25.6% (0.744 [0.722, 0.766]) | 14.0M -> 0.87M | 797 -> 262 MB |
| q11 | 1666 ms | 1346 ms | -18.6% (0.814 [0.783, 0.846]) | 14.0M -> 0.87M | 885 -> 397 MB |
| q4 | 2827 ms | 2470 ms | -14.9% (0.851 [0.830, 0.874]) | 20.0M -> 1.35M | 1561 -> 496 MB |
| All 99 TPC-DS queries, MergeTree (sum of medians, 4 runs per arm) | 77.5 s | 76.9 s | the other 96 queries keep their plans; the largest screen outlier, q7 (+14%), re-measured at 0.991 [0.970, 1.012] | | |
| All 99 TPC-DS queries, Iceberg without statistics (3 pairs) | 93.9 s | 94.2 s | no query slower beyond its 95% CI | | |

<details><summary>Why it happens and what the patch changes</summary>

In q4, q11 and q74, `year_total` is a UNION ALL whose branches set `'s' AS sale_type`, `'w' AS sale_type` (and `'c'` in q4); the outer query picks branches with conditions such as `t_s_firstyear.sale_type = 's'`. Filter push-down replaces the column with each branch's value, which the branch materializes: the selected branch gets `materialize('s') = 's'`, the others `materialize('w') = 's'` and so on. In the selected branch it sits next to `d_year = 2001`, and both reach the `date_dim` read:

```
Prewhere filter column: d_year = 2001 AND d_year IN (2001, 2002)
Filter column: in(d_year, ...) AND materialize('s') = 's' AND equals(d_year, 2001_UInt16)
```

Master already folds a filter that is entirely a constant predicate behind `materialize` (#105987, for #78166: `FilterStep` -> `ActionsDAG::foldFilterPredicateThroughMaterialize` -> `tryFoldPredicate`). That is why the branches that the discriminator rules out are already pruned and estimated empty: the filters on their `customer` and sales-table reads become `Filter column: 0`, and `EXPLAIN` shows `customer[0] ⋈ store_sales[0] ⋈ date_dim[0]` for them. The fold applies only when the whole filter is constant. It does not fold a constant conjunct of an `AND` that also reads a column, so the selected branch keeps `materialize('s') = 's'` next to `d_year = 2001`.

`ConditionSelectivityEstimator::extractAtomFromTree` sees `equals(materialize('s'), 's')`. Its left argument is not a table column, so the estimator applies the default factor for an equality on an expression (1%; 33% for `<` or `>`). The estimate of `date_dim` drops from 358 rows to 3, `store_sales JOIN date_dim` is estimated at about 48 thousand rows instead of about 5.8 million, and join reordering builds that side. `EXPLAIN` of q74 on master:

```
customer[500000] ⋈ (store_sales[28800991] ⋈ date_dim[3])
customer[500000] ⋈ (web_sales[7197566] ⋈ date_dim[3])
```

With the patch, `date_dim` is estimated at 358 rows and `customer` is built again: the hash tables of q74 hold 0.87 million rows instead of 14.0 million.

The patch decides a comparison (`equals`, `notEquals`, `less`, `greater`, `lessOrEquals`, `greaterOrEquals`) whose arguments are both constants, at least one of them behind `materialize`: it compares the two values with `accurateEquals` / `accurateLess` / `accurateLessOrEqual` when they have the same type (after `Nullable` and `LowCardinality`) or are both integers, and marks the condition always true or always false, as the estimator already does for `x = x` (#126131). A comparison with `NULL` is always false. All other conditions are estimated as before.

The patch changes only the estimate: executed filters, results and exceptions stay as they are. It compares `Field`s of the same type (or two integers) instead of extending the fold to conjuncts or calling `tryFoldPredicate`, because evaluating a comparison function on constant arguments can differ from evaluating it per row: #121723 reports wrong results for String vs Enum ordering comparisons folded that way, a case the same-type restriction excludes. Extending the fold to constant conjuncts remains possible as a separate change.

Checks: the results of all 60 timed runs of the three queries are identical between the binaries. `EXPLAIN` of all 99 queries on fresh servers differs only for q4, q11 and q74 (q31 alternates between two runtime-filter placements on both binaries); in those three queries only the selected branches change. The new test `05488_statistics_union_all_constant_column_estimate` shows the estimate of a read in the selected UNION ALL branch (1 row before, 100 rows with the patch, as without the constant column) and of `y = 1 AND materialize(5) > 3` (33 rows before, 100 with the patch). 292 stateless tests about statistics, estimates, join order and UNION ALL give identical output on both binaries, except the new test and one `RuntimeFilterRowsChecked` count in the second run of `05267_join_runtime_filter_prewhere_order_without_statistics` (1000001 vs 1000002), which is identical in three repeated runs of each binary.

The same estimate appears on Iceberg tables with `use_iceberg_manifest_statistics = 1` (#123553, merged on 2026-11-01; the setting is off by default): with that setting, the CI build of #123553 at its final commit `e3a25921` runs q74 on an Iceberg copy of the same data 28% slower (1394 -> 1850 ms) because of it. That combination was not measured with this patch.

</details>

### Which ClickHouse versions are affected?
<details><summary>Tested versions and source commits</summary>

Both arms were built from the same tree (Release, clang-21, ThinLTO off; not an official build): base `8d5702d8ee60fe93378e7830ca1441f2eb05940c` (master, 2026-10-26) and the same commit with this patch. Both include #105987. On master `b1e2520f04f04bff4e249634c005d2c293904d92` (2026-11-01), `extractAtomFromTree`, `RPNBuilder`, `FilterStep.cpp` and the join order search are unchanged since the base; #123553 changed the estimator and join reordering only for Iceberg reads, and the patch applies cleanly. The CI build of master `c189bd99fbda59c78489a6734c49397533bdd591` (2026-11-01, before #123553) shows the same `date_dim[3]` estimate and the same plans for q4, q11 and q74.

</details>

### How to reproduce
<details><summary>Data, queries and measurement procedure</summary>

Data: TPC-DS SF10 generated with `dsdgen` and loaded with `tests/benchmarks/tpc-ds/init.sql` into MergeTree tables with default settings (automatic statistics, `auto_statistics_types = 'basic, uniq_v2'`). Queries: `tests/benchmarks/tpc-ds/queries/query_04.sql` (tpcds-q04), `query_11.sql` (tpcds-q11) and `query_74.sql` (tpcds-q74) with the benchmark settings data_type_default_nullable=1, format_tsv_null_representation='', group_by_use_nulls=1, intersect_default_mode='DISTINCT', joined_subquery_requires_alias=0, join_use_nulls=1, union_default_mode='DISTINCT'.

The estimate alone:

```sql
EXPLAIN SELECT count() FROM store_sales AS ss
INNER JOIN (SELECT d_date_sk FROM date_dim WHERE d_year = 2001 AND materialize('s') = 's') AS d
    ON ss.ss_sold_date_sk = d.d_date_sk;
-- Right: rows estimated 3.00 (358.00 without the materialize condition or with the patch)
```

Measurement: four sessions A B B A over the same data directory (A = base binary, B = patched binary), each with a fresh server; per query one warm-up run and 5 measured runs; `query_duration_ms` from `system.query_log`; run r of the first A session is paired with run r of the first B session, and likewise for the second pair of sessions (10 pairs). The ratio is the geometric mean of the paired ratios with a Student-t 95% interval. The 99-query screen ran each query with one warm-up and two runs per session. Hardware: 4-vCPU arm64 (Neoverse-V1), 30 GB RAM, local disk, warm page cache.

A minimal version without TPC-DS data is the new test in the patch.

</details>

### Expected performance
<details><summary>Expected behavior</summary>

A conjunct that compares a constant with a constant should not lower row estimates: a UNION ALL branch selected by its discriminator should be estimated, and joined, as it is without the discriminator. Branches that the discriminator rules out are already estimated empty on master (#105987).

</details>

### Related issues and pull requests
<details><summary>Related work</summary>

- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/105987 folds a filter that is entirely constant behind `materialize` (for https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/issues/78166), but not a constant conjunct of an `AND`.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/issues/121723 (open): wrong results of that fold for String vs Enum ordering comparisons; https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/127397 (open) proposes a fix.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/126131 estimates the always-true `x = x` in the same function (https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/issues/122677).
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/123553 (merged on 2026-11-01): Iceberg manifest statistics, with which the same estimate makes q74 28% slower.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/issues/128374#issuecomment-6556616287 notes the same q74 estimate in the comparison of Iceberg and MergeTree tables.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/124320 (open) rewrites `extractAtomFromTree` and marks estimates that use default factors, but does not decide constant comparisons; it overlaps textually with this patch.

</details>

### Additional context
<details><summary>Full patch</summary>

```diff
diff --git a/src/Storages/Statistics/ConditionSelectivityEstimator.cpp b/src/Storages/Statistics/ConditionSelectivityEstimator.cpp
index 53e51c36df9..90b2401f928 100644
--- a/src/Storages/Statistics/ConditionSelectivityEstimator.cpp
+++ b/src/Storages/Statistics/ConditionSelectivityEstimator.cpp
@@ -5,6 +5,8 @@

 #include <Common/logger_useful.h>
 #include <Common/ProfileEvents.h>
+#include <Common/FieldAccurateComparison.h>
+#include <Columns/ColumnConst.h>
 #include <DataTypes/DataTypeNothing.h>
 #include <DataTypes/DataTypeNullable.h>
 #include <DataTypes/getLeastSupertype.h>
@@ -286,6 +288,93 @@ bool isAlwaysTrueEquality(const ActionsDAG::Node & node)
         || which.isStringOrFixedString() || which.isUUID() || which.isIPv4() || which.isIPv6();
 }

+/// The value of `node` if it is a constant behind `materialize`. Filter push-down into a branch of UNION ALL
+/// replaces a column that the branch defines as a constant (such as the `'s' AS sale_type` discriminator of
+/// TPC-DS `year_total`) by `materialize(<constant>)`, so a condition on that column compares two constants.
+static bool tryGetMaterializedConstant(const RPNBuilderTreeNode & node, Field & value, DataTypePtr & type)
+{
+    const ActionsDAG::Node * dag_node = node.getDAGNode();
+    if (!dag_node)
+        return false;
+
+    bool unwrapped = false;
+    while (true)
+    {
+        if (dag_node->type == ActionsDAG::ActionType::ALIAS && dag_node->children.size() == 1)
+            dag_node = dag_node->children.front();
+        else if (dag_node->type == ActionsDAG::ActionType::FUNCTION && dag_node->function_base
+                 && dag_node->function_base->getName() == "materialize" && dag_node->children.size() == 1)
+        {
+            dag_node = dag_node->children.front();
+            unwrapped = true;
+        }
+        else
+            break;
+    }
+
+    if (!unwrapped || dag_node->type != ActionsDAG::ActionType::COLUMN || !dag_node->column || !isColumnConst(*dag_node->column))
+        return false;
+
+    value = (*dag_node->column)[0];
+    type = dag_node->result_type;
+    return true;
+}
+
+/// The result of a comparison whose arguments are both constants, at least one of them behind `materialize`, or
+/// nullopt if it cannot be decided here. Only values of one type (or two integers) are compared, for which the
+/// comparison of `Field`s agrees with the comparison function.
+static std::optional<bool> tryEvaluateMaterializedConstantComparison(const String & func_name, const RPNBuilderFunctionTreeNode & func)
+{
+    if (func_name != "equals" && func_name != "notEquals" && func_name != "less" && func_name != "greater"
+        && func_name != "lessOrEquals" && func_name != "greaterOrEquals")
+        return {};
+
+    Field lhs;
+    Field rhs;
+    DataTypePtr lhs_type;
+    DataTypePtr rhs_type;
+    const bool lhs_materialized = tryGetMaterializedConstant(func.getArgumentAt(0), lhs, lhs_type);
+    const bool rhs_materialized = tryGetMaterializedConstant(func.getArgumentAt(1), rhs, rhs_type);
+    if (!lhs_materialized && !rhs_materialized)
+        return {};
+    if ((!lhs_materialized && !func.getArgumentAt(0).tryGetConstant(lhs, lhs_type))
+        || (!rhs_materialized && !func.getArgumentAt(1).tryGetConstant(rhs, rhs_type)))
+        return {};
+
+    /// A comparison with NULL is NULL, which a filter does not pass.
+    if (lhs.isNull() || rhs.isNull())
+        return false;
+    if (!lhs_type || !rhs_type)
+        return {};
+
+    const DataTypePtr lhs_nested = removeLowCardinalityAndNullable(lhs_type);
+    const DataTypePtr rhs_nested = removeLowCardinalityAndNullable(rhs_type);
+    const WhichDataType which(lhs_nested);
+    const bool comparable = which.isStringOrFixedString() || which.isInteger() || which.isDecimal() || which.isEnum()
+        || which.isDateOrDate32OrTimeOrTime64OrDateTimeOrDateTime64() || which.isUUID() || which.isIPv4() || which.isIPv6();
+    if (!comparable || !(lhs_nested->equals(*rhs_nested) || (which.isInteger() && WhichDataType(rhs_nested).isInteger())))
+        return {};
+
+    try
+    {
+        if (func_name == "equals")
+            return accurateEquals(lhs, rhs);
+        if (func_name == "notEquals")
+            return !accurateEquals(lhs, rhs);
+        if (func_name == "less")
+            return accurateLess(lhs, rhs);
+        if (func_name == "greater")
+            return accurateLess(rhs, lhs);
+        if (func_name == "lessOrEquals")
+            return accurateLessOrEqual(lhs, rhs);
+        return accurateLessOrEqual(rhs, lhs);
+    }
+    catch (const Exception &)
+    {
+        return {};
+    }
+}
+
 bool ConditionSelectivityEstimator::extractAtomFromTree(const StorageMetadataPtr & metadata, const RPNBuilderTreeNode & node, RPNElement & out) const
 {
     const auto * node_dag = node.getDAGNode();
@@ -352,6 +441,12 @@ bool ConditionSelectivityEstimator::extractAtomFromTree(const StorageMetadataPtr
                 return true;
             }

+            if (auto result = tryEvaluateMaterializedConstantComparison(func_name, func))
+            {
+                out.function = *result ? RPNElement::ALWAYS_TRUE : RPNElement::ALWAYS_FALSE;
+                return true;
+            }
+
             const bool is_in_operator = functionIsInOperator(func_name);

             /// The right-hand side of IN is a `Set` column: read the elements out of the set. Every other
