MergeTree PREWHERE keeps filtering before `EventDate >= '2013-07-01'` and `URL <> ''` because they count as able to throw; recognizing that they cannot makes ClickBench Q37/Q38 9-21% faster on top of #128442

### Describe the situation
With #128442's patch, MergeTree PREWHERE still filters all columns before `EventDate >= '2013-07-01'` and `URL <> ''`, as both count as able to throw. Neither can: the date literal converts once, and `notEmpty(URL)` reads only string sizes. Fixing that makes ClickBench Q37/Q38 9–21% faster.

Server-side mean latency, #128442 patch vs. plus this patch, x86 4 vCPU, `hits` in one part, 6 paired rounds; change with 95% CI.

| Query, columns cache | #128442, ms | + this patch, ms | Change [95% CI] |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| Q37, cache off | 75.54 | 68.65 | −9.1% [−11.1, −7.0] |
| Q38, cache off | 43.31 | 38.72 | −10.6% [−14.8, −6.3] |
| Q43, cache off | 11.31 | 10.95 | −3.2% [−5.4, −0.9] |
| Q3, cache off (control) | 117.81 | 117.64 | −0.1% [−2.4, +2.2] |
| Q37, cache on | 52.99 | 42.27 | −20.2% [−22.0, −18.5] |
| Q38, cache on | 32.18 | 25.43 | −20.9% [−24.2, −17.6] |
| Q43, cache on | 9.42 | 8.65 | −8.1% [−13.2, −2.7] |
| Q3, cache on (control) | 18.01 | 17.98 | −0.2% [−4.4, +4.2] |

"cache off" is the default; "cache on" is `use_columns_cache = 1`. Q8 and Q39–Q42 do not change significantly (−3.3% to +2.1%).

- On the same rows in 12 parts (merges stopped): Q37 −7.7% / −19.2% and Q38 −11.1% / −23.3% (cache off / on); Q43 −1.8% with the cache off, not significant with it on (−3.9% [−11.8, +4.6]).
- Against master, #128442 together with this patch makes Q37 −8.5% / −20.7% and Q38 −9.9% / −21.3% (one part, cache off / on).
- Memory (median `memory_usage`, one part, cache on): Q38 36.5 → 26.7 MB, Q37 105.0 → 103.3 MB; other queries unchanged.
- Results of Q3, Q8, Q37, Q38 and Q43 are identical on every server of both builds.

<details><summary>Why it happens and what the patch changes</summary>

#128442 keeps the filter of a PREWHERE step only before a step whose condition falls outside the contract of #122508 (cheap, cannot throw, stateless, deterministic). Q37 and Q38 filter on `EventDate >= '2013-07-01' AND EventDate <= '2013-07-31'` and on `URL <> ''` / `Title <> ''`, which become `notEmpty(URL)` / `notEmpty(Title)`. `FunctionComparison::canThrowIfKnown` decides from the argument types only, and comparing a `Date` with a `String` can throw when the string does not parse. `notEmpty` does not implement `canThrowIfKnown`, so it is assumed to throw. These steps therefore still filter all the columns read before them.

A comparison converts a constant string once, before it compares any row (`executeWithConstString`). The patch passes the constant column in `DataTypeWithConstInfo`, and `canThrowIfKnown` performs the same conversion (`convertFieldToType` with the function's format settings). If the literal converts, the rows are compared as values of the column's type, which cannot throw. Enums and types whose own comparison can throw keep the old answer, and so does a literal that does not convert. `empty` and `notEmpty` of strings, arrays, maps, UUID and IP values read only sizes or bytes; that part is the `src/Functions` change of #128342. The places that build `DataTypeWithConstInfo` from constant nodes (`IExecutableFunction::execute`, `ExpressionActions`, `FilterStep`, `lazyFrontier`, `MergeTreeSplitPrewhereIntoReadSteps`) now pass the constant column. `intDiv` and `modulo` already learn their constant divisor another way: `FunctionBinaryArithmeticWithConstants` keeps it from when the function is built, and a comment in `FunctionBinaryArithmetic.h` says that `DataTypesWithConstInfo` does not carry the value, which this patch makes outdated. A maintainer may prefer one mechanism for both.

#122508 uses the same classification to move conditions to the front of `AND`/`OR` (`short_circuit_function_evaluation_reorder_arguments`, on by default). A comparison with a literal that converts can now be moved ahead of a condition that throws on rows it rejects, so such a query stops throwing. For example, on a table where `x = 0` only in rows with `d = '2000-01-01'`, `SELECT count() FROM t WHERE intDiv(10, x) > 1 AND d > '2010-01-01'` throws `ILLEGAL_DIVISION` on master and returns 50 with the patch. With `d > 'abc'`, or with the reordering disabled, both builds throw as before. No case in the checks below started to throw.

Checks, patched build vs master:
- Grouped results of Q37–Q43 identical with the columns cache on and off; Q39–Q42 also differ between servers of the same build, because rows tie at their `LIMIT ... OFFSET`.
- A 28-query differential of comparisons with literals behind guards (`Date`, `Nullable(Date)`, `DateTime`, `DateTime64`, `UUID`, `IPv4`, `Int32`, `String`; PREWHERE, WHERE, `move_all_conditions_to_prewhere`, Log and MergeTree tables): identical except the two reordering cases above and the order shown by `EXPLAIN`.
- 12 guarded queries that throw on the rows an earlier condition rejects (`intDiv` by zero, `toDate`/`toUInt64` of strings, `Map` values, `throwIf`): identical results, no exceptions.
- A new stateless test, `05476_comparison_with_constant_string_cannot_throw` (in the patch), fails on master and passes with the patch, also in 10 runs with random settings.
- Stateless tests matching `short_circuit`, `merge_filters`, `throwif`, `guard`, `prewhere`, `comparison`, `compare_`, `datetime_comparison`, `date_time_compar`, `string_to_date` and `reorder`, including `02473_multistep_prewhere`, `02473_multistep_split_prewhere` and the nine `00411_long_accurate_number_comparison_*` tests: the same results as master; the only failures, on both builds, need the stateful `test.hits` dataset, a cluster or other services missing from the test server. The queries of `00072_compare_date_and_string_index` give identical results on a small `test.hits`.

</details>

### Which ClickHouse versions are affected?
<details><summary>Tested versions and source commits</summary>

Master `e4afc4fd7f50f14b0bb11ad83a9c0b85f3cc1b59` (26.11) with the patch from #128442, vs. the same plus the patch below. No later commit on master, up to `c189bd99fbda59c78489a6734c49397533bdd591`, changes the patched files.

</details>

### How to reproduce
<details><summary>Data, queries and measurement procedure</summary>

- Data and procedure: as in #128442. ClickBench `hits` with the official `create.sql` plus `auto_statistics_types = ''`, 99,997,497 rows; merged into one part with `OPTIMIZE TABLE hits FINAL`, and the 12-part table before merging with merges stopped.
- Queries: lines 3, 8 and 37–43 of ClickBench `queries.sql`, default settings (4 threads), `use_columns_cache = 1` for the "cache on" rows:

```sql
SELECT URL, COUNT(*) AS PageViews FROM hits WHERE CounterID = 62 AND EventDate >= '2013-07-01' AND EventDate <= '2013-07-31' AND DontCountHits = 0 AND IsRefresh = 0 AND URL <> '' GROUP BY URL ORDER BY PageViews DESC LIMIT 10; -- Q37
SELECT Title, COUNT(*) AS PageViews FROM hits WHERE CounterID = 62 AND EventDate >= '2013-07-01' AND EventDate <= '2013-07-31' AND DontCountHits = 0 AND IsRefresh = 0 AND Title <> '' GROUP BY Title ORDER BY PageViews DESC LIMIT 10; -- Q38
SELECT DATE_TRUNC('minute', EventTime) AS M, COUNT(*) AS PageViews FROM hits WHERE CounterID = 62 AND EventDate >= '2013-07-14' AND EventDate <= '2013-07-15' AND IsRefresh = 0 AND DontCountHits = 0 GROUP BY DATE_TRUNC('minute', EventTime) ORDER BY DATE_TRUNC('minute', EventTime) LIMIT 10 OFFSET 1000; -- Q43
```

- Hardware and build: AWS x86 4 vCPU (Xeon 8375C), 30 GiB; Release build, clang 21, both binaries from the same build directory.
- Measurement: two servers per binary on hard-linked copies of the data. In each round, for each server in rotated order and each query, `clickhouse benchmark -c 1 -i 30 --query "..."` after 15 warm-up runs. Latency is the mean server-side duration of the 30 runs from `system.query_log` (`QueryFinish`), then the geometric mean over the two servers of a binary. The change is the geometric mean of the per-round ratios with a 95% t-interval, 6 rounds on each layout.
- Memory: median `memory_usage` from `system.query_log` over the measured runs.

</details>

### Expected performance
<details><summary>Expected behavior</summary>

A comparison with a literal that converts to the column's type, and `empty`/`notEmpty` of a string, cannot throw, so they should not force PREWHERE to filter all columns before them or keep #122508 from evaluating them first.

</details>

### Related issues and pull requests
<details><summary>Related work</summary>

- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/issues/128442 — the PREWHERE step filter this patch builds on; measured on top of its patch.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/128342 — its `src/Functions` change (`empty`/`notEmpty` `canThrowIfKnown`) is included in the patch below, because Q37/Q38 need both kinds of condition classified.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/122508 — the argument reordering that uses the same `canThrow` classification.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/125978 — changes `comparisonCanThrow` for `Tuple` arguments; this patch changes `canThrowIfKnown`, which calls it, so the two compose. Until #125978 lands, comparing a `Tuple` column with `Variant` or `Dynamic` elements to a string literal that converts also counts as unable to throw with this patch, so such a guarded condition could throw where master answers, as #128442 notes for two such columns (derived from the source, not reproduced).
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/127397 — changes how `Enum` columns compare with strings; this patch leaves `Enum` comparisons classified as before.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/issues/128432 — drops columns that later PREWHERE steps do not need, and also makes Q37/Q38 faster (one part, against master: Q37 −3.4% and Q38 −3.9% by default, −2.8% and −6.0% with the columns cache). With this patch, the steps before the `EventDate` and `notEmpty` conditions no longer filter, so the two gains on Q37/Q38 partly overlap and do not add up. #128432 was measured against master and this patch on top of #128442; they were not measured together.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/issues/128347 — keeps the rows a PREWHERE selects in the query condition cache, so a repeated query skips these steps. It numbers queries from 0: its repeated-run Q36 (`URL`, ratio 0.794) and Q37 (`Title`, 0.763) are Q37/Q38 here. All numbers in this report are from repeated runs; where #128347 applies, the two gains do not add up. First runs and queries whose condition is not cached still evaluate these steps, so this patch still applies to them.

</details>

