| Q17 | 0.991 [0.960, 1.024] | 0.991 [0.961, 1.022] | 1.000 [0.957, 1.045] | 1.025 [1.013, 1.037] |
| E1 | 1.016 [1.002, 1.031] | 1.006 [0.987, 1.025] | 1.004 [0.997, 1.012] | 1.014 [1.004, 1.024] |
| Q2 | 0.998 [0.971, 1.026] | 0.999 [0.981, 1.017] | 0.994 [0.976, 1.012] | 0.999 [0.974, 1.024] |

Results are identical in every run. Peak memory is unchanged (S1: 17.0 MiB cold; 21.1 vs 21.2 MiB warm).

<details><summary>Why it happens and what the patch changes</summary>

`MergeTreeReaderStream` sizes the read buffer of a column as `min(max_read_buffer_size_local_fs, largest mark range in bytes)`. When index analysis leaves both long runs and isolated granules in a part, every seek to an isolated granule reads a whole 128 KiB buffer, although Q19 needs only about 19 KiB of compressed data per granule (42.3 MiB for 2,224 granules). In the cold Q19 runs the server requests 65.4 MiB from the files to decompress 42.3 MiB, and the disk delivers 75.5 MiB.

Since #122250, every MergeTree stream can turn its part's mark ranges into the byte ranges it will read (`ReadBuffer::setRequestMap`), but the map is created only with `use_reader_executor`, and only the experimental `ReaderExecutor` uses it (#125606 bounds its fetches by it). The default local readers never see it.

The patch:
- creates the request map also for parts on local disks (`MergeTreeReadPoolBase`). #122250 creates it only with `use_reader_executor`, so its map code (refined maps, parallel-replica and patch-part maps) now also runs for local parts;
- makes a stream of a local file announce, at each seek, only the ranges of the map that a read starting there can reach: the ranges that begin less than one read buffer after the end of the previous one, at most 64. Later seeks inside these ranges reuse them (`MergeTreeReaderStream`);
- makes `ReadBufferFromFileDescriptor` (`pread`) and `AsynchronousReadBufferFromFileDescriptor` (`pread_threadpool`, the default) end each read at the end of the last announced range that begins inside the buffer window. Every requested byte that a full-size read would fetch is still fetched by the same read, so the number of reads does not grow, dense ranges are read as before, and reads outside the map or with `O_DIRECT` keep the full size. A read is never shorter than 1 byte.

Two simpler versions were worse. Converting the whole map for every stream of every reader, as for the reader executor, made the warm S1 43% slower at 4 threads (65.5 → 93.5 ms, user CPU +15%). Ending every read at the end of its own range, which also skips the small gaps between nearby granules, was faster at 3000 IOPS but issued more reads (Q19: 777 → 1124 file reads, Q2: 730 → 1035), which costs time where the number of I/O operations rather than bandwidth is the limit. This patch never splits a read.

The map holds the ranges that primary-key analysis selects. Granules dropped later, by skip indexes evaluated during reading (the default, `use_skip_indexes_on_data_read = 1`), by PREWHERE results or by the `GROUP BY ... LIMIT` key threshold, stay inside these ranges, so the reads around them keep the full size: Q17 and E1 read the same bytes. The memory cost is at most 64 ranges of 16 bytes per stream.

Correctness: results are identical in every run of both binaries. A differential test of 80 queries (sparse PREWHERE, top-K and lazy reads over granules of 64 rows sharing compressed blocks, adaptive granularity and sparse serialization; `pread`, `read`, `mmap`, `O_DIRECT`, 4 KiB buffers, uncompressed cache, prefetch) returns the same results as master. 584 stateless tests about local reads, PREWHERE, skip indexes, compact parts and LowCardinality give the same outcomes as master, with default settings (483 pass, counting 02202, which collides with 02201 when run in sequence and passes alone; 101 fail on both) and with every part forced wide (480 pass, 104 fail on both). The failures on both binaries need the CI cluster configuration. They include the request-map tests (05257-05261, 05317) and the parallel-replica tests, so the map code that this patch now also runs for local parts is not covered by this run.

</details>

### Which ClickHouse versions are affected?
<details><summary>Tested versions and source commits</summary>

master `6983f5a16d92c831f350e3aa9995a7a1d6f08298` (2026-10-31, 26.11.1.1) against the same tree with the patch (`c366a0ce95ac`). Release build, clang 21, no LTO, AWS r7g.xlarge (Graviton3, 4 vCPU, 30 GiB) with an EBS root volume. The read path is unchanged on master up to `47f313c2392f` (2026-10-31 19:15 UTC).

</details>

### How to reproduce
<details><summary>Data, queries and measurement procedure</summary>

Data: ClickBench `hits` (99,997,497 rows) in 2 parts with merges stopped, as table `hits_mm`: the same data with `minmax` skip indexes (GRANULARITY 1) on the numeric and date columns. Only the `EventTime` index is used, by the query that sets `use_skip_indexes = 1`; all other queries run with `use_skip_indexes = 0`, so they read like the plain ClickBench table.

Cold: for each query and round, and for each binary in alternating order: stop the server, `sync`, `echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches`, start the server inside a cgroup with `io.max` `rbps=125829120 riops=3000` on the data device, run the query 3 times (the first run is the cold run), then hash the TSV output of a 4th run. 6 rounds. Statistic: cold `query_duration_ms`; per-round ratio patched/master, geometric mean with a Student-t 95% interval. Disk bytes: `OSReadBytes`; file reads: `ThreadPoolReaderPageCacheHit` + `ThreadPoolReaderPageCacheMiss`; requested bytes: `ReadBufferFromFileDescriptorReadBytes`; disk reads: `rios` of the cgroup's `io.stat`.

Dropping the page cache also evicts the server binary, so the cold runs read code from disk as well. The freshly built patched binary was more fragmented on disk (716 extents vs 72), which added 50-200 disk reads to every patched run. Yet in the same run every control came out 0-6.4% faster with the patch (Q17 -6.4% [-10.4%, -2.2%], Q36 -2.7% [-3.9%, -1.4%]), with disk bytes and file reads within 2% of master. The read change does not explain that, so it is not known whether reading the binaries biases the table for or against the patch. A second run agrees with the table. There, each binary was read back into the page cache after `drop_caches` (`cat clickhouse > /dev/null`), so that only the data was cold. At 3000 IOPS, Q19 was 0.786 [0.694, 0.891] and S1 0.801 [0.726, 0.885], and no control changed significantly. Median disk reads were at most 3.2% above master for any query (Q40: 77.5 → 80) and lower for Q19 (696 → 654) and S1 (1159 → 1113). The 1000 IOPS numbers above come from this setup. Its latencies jump between throttling levels (for example 450 or 615 ms for Q17), which widens its intervals.

Warm: page cache warm, `use_query_condition_cache = 0`, a fresh server per binary and round, one untimed run, then 5 timed runs (per-round median); `max_threads` default (4) and 16; latency and `UserTimeMicroseconds` from `system.query_log`; 6 rounds.

