Filtering a sparse column makes one countBytesInFilter call per run of default rows; counting along 64-row filter words makes native ClickBench Q42/Q43 5-7% faster at defaults

### Describe the situation
Filtering a sparse column counts the kept rows before each non-default value with one `countBytesInFilter` call per run of default rows. For flag columns with 2-7% non-default values these runs are a few dozen rows, so the calls dominate. Counting along 64-row filter bitmask words removes them.

Server-side query duration from `system.query_log`, native ClickBench `hits`, x86 4 vCPU, warm, master vs patched, 95% CI.

| Query (`queries.sql` line), columns cache | Master, ms | Patched, ms | Change [95% CI] |
|---|---:|---:|---|
