| Q43, cache on | 9.88 | 9.52 | −3.7% [−8.0, +0.8] |
| Controls: Q3, Q10, Q37, Q38, Q40, Q41 (cache on and off); Q39 (off); Q30, EXPLAIN Q43 (on) | | | −1.0% to +2.1%, none significant |
| Q8 `AdvEngineID ... WHERE AdvEngineID <> 0`, cache off / on | 18.09 / 14.48 | 18.28 / 14.68 | +1.0% [0.0, +2.1] / +1.3% [−3.5, +6.3] |
| 4 clients, Q39/Q42/Q43 mix, cache on | 126.0 QPS | 134.0 QPS | +6.3% (medians of 4 rounds) |

<details><summary>Why it happens and what the patch changes</summary>

`ColumnSparse::filter` (both overloads) walks the offsets of the non-default rows and, since #105890, adds `countBytesInFilter(filt, prev, row)` for the run of default rows before each of them. That is one `countBytesInFilter` call per non-default row, which mostly runs its scalar tail: a run shorter than 64 rows never enters its 64-byte vector loop and goes to the byte-wise tail loop. The flag columns of the dashboard queries are stored sparse in all or most parts of the official load (default `ratio_of_defaults_for_sparse_serialization = 0.9375`): in the `CounterID = 62` rows, `IsRefresh` has 2.1%, `DontCountHits` 6.9% and `IsLink` 6.8% non-default values, so their runs of defaults average about 15-50 rows. This cost recurs in every PREWHERE step that filters these columns (`MergeTreeRangeReader::filterColumns`). Q11/Q12 gain for the same reason: their filter column `MobilePhoneModel` is also stored sparse (about 6% non-empty).

The patch walks the offsets with a cursor over 64-row words of the filter (`bytes64MaskToBits64Mask`): the number of kept rows before a non-default row is the popcount of the words before it plus the popcount of its own word below it. A run that spans whole words is still counted with one `countBytesInFilter` call, so very sparse columns do the same work as before. The result columns are unchanged.

CPU profile (query profiler, warm loop, columns cache on): for Q43, `ColumnSparse::filter` takes 9.5% of all samples, including its `countBytesInFilter` calls, with the official CI `amd_release` build (ThinLTO) of master 3c82fa8c, and 4.0% with the patch in our non-LTO build. For Q39, with both binaries built the same way (non-LTO), 12.9% + 2.2% (in-place overload) becomes 6.5% + 1.8%.

Correctness:
- Hashes of the complete grouped results (no `ORDER BY`/`LIMIT`) of Q37-Q43, Q2, Q8, Q20, Q21 and Q31 are identical between master and the patch, with the columns cache on and off; Q10-Q12 return identical results.
- A differential script compares 9 filter queries over a sparse table and an identical non-sparse table (`ratio_of_defaults_for_sparse_serialization` 0.5 vs 1.0; columns with 0.3%, 1%, 3%, 10% and 30% non-default values, an all-default column, `Nullable` and `String`): byte-identical output on master and the patch, and sparse = non-sparse.
- The 108 stateless tests with `sparse` in the name give the same status on master and the patch (101 OK; the same 7 fail in both because the scratch server has no S3/remote setup).
- Write path: `INSERT ... SELECT` with a filter over sparse columns, `ALTER TABLE ... DELETE` and `OPTIMIZE FINAL` on a 20M-row table give identical results; the script takes 3.31-3.35 s with the patch and 3.37-3.46 s on master (3 runs each).

</details>

### Which ClickHouse versions are affected?
<details><summary>Tested versions and source commits</summary>

Master `e4afc4fd7f50f14b0bb11ad83a9c0b85f3cc1b59` (2026-10-31) and the same commit with the patch below. Release builds, clang-21, without ThinLTO; the Q43 master profile above uses the official CI `amd_release` build (ThinLTO) of the parent commit `3c82fa8cc9b8f0a604c22e7fb7ea1d1259a676d2`. No later master commit, up to `33dac5dbc59d97ca60162747d7edb528f63980a5`, changes `src/Columns/`, `MergeTreeRangeReader` or `MergeTreeReadersChain`.

