<!-- ch-version-info:start -->
### Version info
- Resolved by: #128359
- Merged into: `26.11.1.1050-master` (included in `26.11` and later)
<!-- ch-version-info:end -->

Parquet: bit-packed dictionary indexes are unpacked one value at a time with a run-time bit width; a width-specialized loop makes ClickBench Q30, Q3 and Q4 9% faster

### Describe the situation
The Parquet reader unpacks bit-packed dictionary indexes one value at a time, with the bit width known only at run time and the bit position written back to memory after every value. A loop specialized per bit width makes scans of dictionary-encoded columns about 9% faster.

One-shot clickhouse local on ClickBench hits.parquet, 4-core Graviton3, 10 interleaved rounds; time ratio with 95% CI.

| Query (median of 10 runs) | master | patched | patched / master [95% CI] |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| Q30 `SUM(ResolutionWidth + k)`, k = 0..89 | 174.0 ms | 157.0 ms | 0.907 [0.879, 0.936] |
| Q3 `SUM(AdvEngineID), COUNT(*), AVG(ResolutionWidth)` | 196.0 ms | 177.0 ms | 0.910 [0.890, 0.930] |
| Q4 `AVG(UserID)` | 244.0 ms | 221.5 ms | 0.905 [0.888, 0.921] |
| Q16 `UserID, COUNT(*) ... GROUP BY UserID` | 363.5 ms | 341.0 ms | 0.947 [0.930, 0.965] |
| Q13, Q33 | 1109.5 / 1756.0 ms | 1092.0 / 1724.0 ms | 0.984 [0.973, 0.996] / 0.983 [0.967, 1.000] |
| Q2, Q7 (controls, 60 and 9 ms) | 61.0 / 9.0 ms | 62.0 / 9.0 ms | 1.045 [0.846, 1.292] / 0.990 [0.921, 1.063] |
| With #124859 in both builds: Q30 / Q3 / Q4 | 153.5 / 171.5 / 213.0 ms | 145.0 / 167.0 / 207.5 ms | 0.940 / 0.964 / 0.966, intervals below 1 |

<details><summary>Why it happens and what the patch changes</summary>

`BitPackedRLEDecoder` in `src/Processors/Formats/Impl/Parquet/Decoding.cpp` decodes the dictionary indexes of every dictionary-encoded column and the definition and repetition levels. In a bit-packed run it reads each value with an 8-byte load, a shift and a mask computed from the run-time `bit_width`, and advances the member `bit_idx` after every value. The loads go through `const char *`, so the compiler must assume they can read `this->bit_idx` and stores the cursor to memory on every value. In a perf profile of one-shot Q30 on Graviton3, `decodeAndIndex` has 13% of the samples, 42% of them on the instruction that stores the cursor; `decodeAndIndex`, `appendIndexes`, `appendRepeated` and `startRun` together take 29-34% of Q3, Q4 and Q30.

The patch adds `unpackBitPacked<W>`, instantiated for each width (1-32 for indexes, 1-8 for levels) and chosen once per page from a table. It keeps the bit position in a local variable, decodes each byte-aligned block of 8 values with constant offsets and shifts, and bounds-checks a batch in a separate loop that vectorizes. It reads the same bytes as before (an 8-byte load at the first byte of each value), so it needs no extra padding. The filtered decode path is unchanged.

Q2's wide interval comes from three rounds with 95-109 ms runs that hit all builds alike (medians 61 and 62 ms).

Correctness: all 43 ClickBench queries return the same results in both builds. Eight queries whose `LIMIT`/`OFFSET` picks among ties differ between runs of either build at default threads; they are identical with `max_threads = 1`. A sweep of index widths 1-18 with Nullable and Array columns, three block sizes and `PREWHERE` matches a `numbers()` oracle in both builds; the new test 05482 covers it.

Costs, same host: across the 43-query suite (3 pairs per query) no query is significantly slower. Peak RSS is the same (`SELECT 1` 185.6-186.0 MB, Q30 289.7-296.0 MB in both builds). Data-cold runs (page cache dropped) are I/O-bound and unchanged: Q4 1.585-1.592 s vs 1.587-1.600 s, Q30 0.33-0.38 s in both. Four concurrent Q30 processes take 0.512-0.527 s vs 0.481-0.498 s.

</details>

### Which ClickHouse versions are affected?
<details><summary>Tested versions and source commits</summary>

master 4865d752f399 (2026-10-27). The Parquet reader is unchanged on master up to 80eb86bf2a2d apart from a macro rename. Local Release build with clang-21, without ThinLTO, on aarch64 (Graviton3, 4 cores, 30 GiB).

</details>

### How to reproduce
<details><summary>Data, queries and measurement procedure</summary>

Data: the single-file ClickBench `hits.parquet` (https://datasets.clickhouse.com/hits_compatible/hits.parquet) with the `create.sql` of ClickBench's `clickhouse-parquet` entry (`ENGINE = File(Parquet, 'hits.parquet')`) and its `queries.sql`.

Each run is a fresh process, as in ClickBench's `clickhouse-parquet/query` script:

```bash
./clickhouse local --time --format=Null --query="$(cat create.sql) $(sed -n 30p queries.sql)"
```

The page cache is warm. For each query, every build runs once as a warm-up; then 10 rounds run all builds (master, patched, master + #124859, master + #124859 + this change) in rotating order. The ratio is the geometric mean of the per-round ratios, with a Student-t 95% interval.

Measurement IDs of the table rows: Q30 `clickbench-q30-parquet-single-file-oneshot`, Q3 `clickbench-q3-parquet-single-file-oneshot`, Q4 `clickbench-q4-parquet-single-file-oneshot`, Q16 `clickbench-q16-parquet-single-file-oneshot`, Q13 `clickbench-q13-parquet-single-file-oneshot-control`, Q33 `clickbench-q33-parquet-single-file-oneshot-control`, Q2 `clickbench-q2-parquet-single-file-oneshot-control`, Q7 `clickbench-q7-parquet-single-file-oneshot-control`.

</details>

### Expected performance
<details><summary>Expected behavior</summary>

Bit-packed indexes should be unpacked in a few instructions per value without a memory store per value, as width-specialized unpackers do (for example Arrow's `unpack32`), so that scans of dictionary-encoded Parquet columns are limited by decompression and by the consumer rather than by index decoding.

</details>

### Related issues and pull requests
<details><summary>Related work</summary>

- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/124859 changes the same function (one column resize per call and a fused gather for fixed-size dictionaries) but keeps the per-value unpack with a run-time width. The two changes add up: with #124859 in both builds this change gives the 0.94-0.97 row above, and together they make Q30, Q3 and Q4 15-16% faster than master. On top of #124859, the same `unpack_indexes` call replaces the per-value loop in its `decodeAndIndexFixedSize`: unpack up to 512 indexes into a stack buffer, bounds-check the batch, then gather.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/126477 adds a width-specialized `unpackBits` for `DELTA_BINARY_PACKED` and ALP while removing the Arrow dependency; it does not change `BitPackedRLEDecoder`. If it merges first, this patch can call that function instead of adding `unpackBitPacked` (its `bit_stream_utils_internal.h` include removal touches the same include block).
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/issues/124666
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/115819

</details>

