### Version info
- Resolved by: #128262
- Merged into: `26.11.1.1089-master` (included in `26.11` and later)
<!-- ch-version-info:end -->

JOIN runtime filters on Nullable integer keys look up 128-bit packed keys; a plain value set with a NULL flag makes Iceberg TPC-DS q58, q96 and q98 13-16% faster

### Describe the situation
A JOIN runtime filter with an exact key set builds its `Set` with `transform_null_in` when the key is `Nullable`, so every probe row is packed with a NULL flag into a 128-bit key. A set of the plain values plus a flag for a NULL key gives the same results with a cheaper lookup.

TPC-DS SF10 Iceberg tables, 4 vCPUs, warm cache: median of 7 paired runs; patched/master ratio with Student-t 95% CI.

| Query | master, ms | patched, ms | patched / master (95% CI) |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| q98 | 376 | 333 | 0.840 (0.780 to 0.905) |
| q58 | 649 | 558 | 0.858 (0.836 to 0.880) |
| q96 | 462 | 398 | 0.872 (0.833 to 0.912) |
| q88 | 3705 | 3189 | 0.884 (0.823 to 0.951) |
| q14 (both statements) | 10426 | 9391 | 0.900 (0.891 to 0.909) |
| q43 | 737 | 662 | 0.909 (0.883 to 0.937) |
| q36 | 1096 | 1023 | 0.946 (0.926 to 0.966) |
| Controls q03, q21, q37, q52, q82 | | | 0.974 to 1.010; every interval includes 1 |
| q42 control, patched binary on the second / first server | 215 / 217 | 218 / 211 | 1.019 (1.000 to 1.037) / 0.960 (0.928 to 0.994) |
| q96 / q58 with the servers swapped, 9 runs | 459 / 644 | 394 / 554 | 0.854 (0.827 to 0.882) / 0.856 (0.842 to 0.870) |
| All 99 queries, 3 paired runs | 174.4 s in total | 168.9 s | 25 faster and none slower (95% CI); geometric mean 0.955 |
| q72, the noisiest query in that screen, 9 runs per server placement | 4137 / 4002 | 4071 / 4029 | 1.010 (0.913 to 1.117) / 0.974 (0.905 to 1.048) |
| 4 concurrent clients, 13-query mix, 2 blocks per binary | 173.5 s, 172.8 s per block | 158.9 s, 157.0 s | 0.912 |
| OS page cache dropped before each run: q88 / q96, 5 runs | 4141 / 1868 | 3656 / 1862 | 0.882 (0.872 to 0.893) / 0.993 (0.987 to 1.000); six others 0.988 to 1.007 |
| MergeTree TPC-DS (same schema, decompression-bound): q88 / q96, 5 runs | 4174 / 520 | 4159 / 518 | 0.990 (0.970 to 1.010) / 0.996 (0.963 to 1.030); all 13 queries unchanged |
| Synthetic MergeTree join on a `Nullable(Int64)` key | 891 | 824 | 0.944 (0.906 to 0.983) |
| Same join on an `Int64` key (reference) | 810 | 813 | 0.980 (0.924 to 1.040) |
| Peak memory per query | | | unchanged |

<details><summary>Why it happens and what the patch changes</summary>

`ExactSetRuntimeFilter` creates `Set(SizeLimits{}, -1, /* transform_null_in = */ argument_can_have_nulls)`. For a `Nullable(Int64)` key this selects the `nullable_keys128` method: each probed row is packed with its NULL flag into a 128-bit key and looked up in a 128-bit hash table. Every optional Iceberg or Parquet column is `Nullable` in ClickHouse, and so is every column of the TPC-DS benchmark schema not declared `NOT NULL` (`data_type_default_nullable = 1`), so fact-table foreign keys take this path even when no value is NULL. In a `perf record` of q96 on master, `Set::executeImplCase<SetMethodKeysFixed<UInt128>>` and `HashMethodKeysFixed<UInt128, nullable>` take about 24% of server CPU.

The patch changes only `Nullable` keys whose nested type is represented by an integer (`Int*`, `UInt*`, `Date`, `DateTime`, `Enum`, `IPv4`). It builds the set without `transform_null_in`, which keeps the non-NULL values in a one-number hash table, and remembers whether the build side inserted a NULL key. After the lookup, a NULL probe row gets the result of a set member only if a NULL key was inserted, exactly as with `transform_null_in`. `getValuesColumn` returns the values with the key type again, including the NULL key, so index analysis, merging of partial filters and transported filters see the same values as before. The NULL key and the null-map bytes still count against `join_runtime_filter_exact_values_limit` and the byte limit, so the switch to a Bloom filter happens at the same point. Other key types (`Decimal`, `String`, floats, `LowCardinality(Nullable)`) are unchanged.

User CPU falls with latency: q88 12.9 s to 10.9 s, q58 1.97 s to 1.63 s, q96 1.61 s to 1.35 s per run. Small queries move by 2 to 4% depending on which of the two servers runs which binary, so q96, q58 and q42 were also measured with the servers swapped. On the MergeTree copy of the same benchmark the patch changes neither latency nor CPU: there q96 spends about 70% of its CPU in ZSTD decompression and the set lookup does not show up in the profile. In the synthetic MergeTree join, where the lookup is a visible share, the patch brings the `Nullable` key to the cost of the plain `Int64` key (824 ms vs 813 ms).

Correctness, master vs patched:
- 210 join queries (`=`, `<=>`, LEFT SEMI, LEFT ANTI, RIGHT, `IN`, `NOT IN`) over build sides with and without NULLs, a single value, a single value plus NULL, empty, only NULLs and 30,000 keys. Key types: `Nullable` `Int64`, `Int32`, `UInt8`, `UInt128`, `Date`, `DateTime`, `Enum8`, `IPv4`, mixed `Int32`/`Int64`, a non-`Nullable` probe, and the unchanged `Decimal(18, 2)`, `String` and `LowCardinality(Nullable(Int64))`. Each with runtime filters on and off and `join_runtime_filter_exact_values_limit` 10000 and 1: one result set across all 8 combinations.
- The 72 `tests/queries/0_stateless/*runtime_filter*.sql` tests run with `clickhouse local`: identical output.
- Every timed query: identical complete results on both servers.
- Not run: transported runtime filters of distributed plans. They serialize `getValuesColumn`, which returns the same values as before, and the receiver's limit check sees the same count.

</details>

### Which ClickHouse versions are affected?
<details><summary>Tested versions and source commits</summary>

Master 4865d752f3991c7e53e22d146250f2b8ec3d8ef8 (26.11.1.1, 2026-10-27), Release build with clang 21; the patched binary is the same tree and flags plus the patch below. Current master d350a633d54015ae4a6f44592309b07974a6d019 has no changes to `RuntimeFilterLookup`, `Set`, the hash methods or the Parquet and Iceberg readers since then.

</details>

### How to reproduce
<details><summary>Data, queries and measurement procedure</summary>

Self-contained check (any table engine; here MergeTree), the same join with a `Nullable(Int64)` and an `Int64` key:

```sql
CREATE TABLE probe_n (k Nullable(Int64), v UInt64) ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY v;
CREATE TABLE probe_p (k Int64, v UInt64) ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY v;
CREATE TABLE build_n (k Nullable(Int64)) ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY tuple();
CREATE TABLE build_p (k Int64) ENGINE = MergeTree ORDER BY tuple();
INSERT INTO probe_n SELECT intHash64(number) % 2000000, number FROM numbers(50000000);
INSERT INTO probe_p SELECT intHash64(number) % 2000000, number FROM numbers(50000000);
INSERT INTO build_n SELECT number * 400 FROM numbers(5000);
INSERT INTO build_p SELECT number * 400 FROM numbers(5000);
OPTIMIZE TABLE probe_n FINAL;
OPTIMIZE TABLE probe_p FINAL;

SELECT count(), sum(v) FROM probe_n AS p INNER JOIN build_n AS b ON p.k = b.k;  -- Nullable key
SELECT count(), sum(v) FROM probe_p AS p INNER JOIN build_p AS b ON p.k = b.k;  -- reference
```

TPC-DS on Iceberg:

1. Load TPC-DS SF10 (`dsdgen -scale 10`) into the tables of [`tests/benchmarks/tpc-ds/init.sql`](https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/blob/master/tests/benchmarks/tpc-ds/init.sql) (database `tpcds`; with `data_type_default_nullable = 1`, every column not declared `NOT NULL`, including the fact-table foreign keys, is `Nullable`).
2. Write each table with ClickHouse as an Iceberg table (`UInt32` and `FixedString` changed to `Int64` and `String`), sorted by its MergeTree sorting key:

```bash
for t in $(clickhouse-client -q "SELECT name FROM system.tables WHERE database = 'tpcds'"); do
  pk=$(clickhouse-client -q "SELECT sorting_key FROM system.tables WHERE database = 'tpcds' AND name = '$t'")
  cols=$(clickhouse-client -q "SELECT arrayStringConcat(arrayMap(x -> x.2, arraySort(groupArray((position, concat(name, ' ', multiIf(type LIKE 'Nullable(FixedString%', 'Nullable(String)', type LIKE 'FixedString%', 'String', type = 'UInt32', 'Int64', type = 'Nullable(UInt32)', 'Nullable(Int64)', type)))))), ', ') FROM system.columns WHERE database = 'tpcds' AND table = '$t'")
  sel=$(clickhouse-client -q "SELECT arrayStringConcat(arrayMap(x -> x.2, arraySort(groupArray((position, multiIf(type LIKE '%FixedString%', concat('toStringCutToZero(', name, ')'), type LIKE '%UInt32%', concat('toInt64(', name, ')'), name))))), ', ') FROM system.columns WHERE database = 'tpcds' AND table = '$t'")
  clickhouse-client -q "CREATE TABLE iceds.$t ($cols) ENGINE = IcebergLocal('/data/iceds/$t/', 'Parquet')"
  clickhouse-client --allow_insert_into_iceberg 1 --max_insert_threads 4 -q "INSERT INTO iceds.$t SELECT $sel FROM tpcds.$t ORDER BY $pk"
done
```

3. Start a master server and a patched server on the same read-only files. Run `tests/benchmarks/tpc-ds/queries/query_NN.sql` against database `iceds` with the settings of `tests/benchmarks/tpc-ds/settings.json` plus `use_hash_table_stats_for_join_reordering = 0` and `collect_hash_table_stats_during_joins = 0`, which keep the join order the same in every run. Runtime filters stay at their defaults.
4. Per query: one warmup on each server (complete TSV result, compared between servers), then 7 paired runs in ABBA order. The metric is `query_duration_ms` from `system.query_log`; for the two-statement q14 it is the sum of both. The ratio is the geometric mean of the paired patched/master ratios with a Student-t 95% interval.

5. Concurrency: 4 clients each run the 13 queries of the first table twice, in rotated order; blocks run master, patched, patched, master, one binary at a time; the metric is the wall time of a block. OS-cold: `sync; echo 3 > /proc/sys/vm/drop_caches` before every timed run, after one discarded cold run per binary.

Host: one 4-vCPU x86-64 VM with 30 GiB RAM, local disk, warm page cache unless stated.

Identifiers of the measured series in the attached evidence: tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q98, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q58, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q96, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q88, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q14, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q43, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q36, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q03, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q21, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q37, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q52, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q82, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q42, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q42-swapped, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q58-swapped, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q96-swapped, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q72-recheck, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q72-recheck-swapped, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q88-oscold, tpcds-sf10-iceberg-q96-oscold, tpcds-sf10-mergetree-q88, tpcds-sf10-mergetree-q96, synthetic-nullable-int64-join, synthetic-int64-join.

</details>

### Expected performance
<details><summary>Expected behavior</summary>

A runtime filter on a `Nullable` integer key should cost about the same per row as on the same key without `Nullable`: a lookup of the plain value, with NULL rows decided by the null map.

</details>

### Related issues and pull requests
<details><summary>Related work</summary>

- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/109240 (closed without merge) also targeted runtime filters on `Nullable` keys. It kept the `transform_null_in` set for the exact filter and changed NULL handling around the lookup and in the Bloom filter. This patch removes the packed-key lookup itself.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/96779 (open) adds a min/max range check in front of Bloom-filter runtime filters for numeric keys. It touches the same file but not the exact set, so the two are independent.
- https://github.com/ClickHouse/ClickHouse/pull/124391 (open) skips the identity `accurateOrNull` cast in `Set::execute` for non-`Nullable` probes. It is complementary: this patch changes which set a `Nullable` key uses.

</details>

